    "body": "CSX’s third-quarter profits increased due to a combination of volume growth, higher fuel surcharge revenue, and efficiency gains, the railroad said.\nOperating income increased 44%, to $1.57 billion, while revenue increased 12%, to a record $4.02 billion, the company said Wednesday. Earnings per share increased 54%, to 57 cents. Adjusting for the impact of a one-time goodwill impairment in the third quarter of 2025, operating income was up 25% and earnings per share rose 30%.\nThe railroad’s (NASDAQ: CSX ) operating ratio was 60.9%, an 8.8-point improvement from a year ago, as expenses declined 2%. CSX set what it called an all-time U.S. quarterly record for fuel efficiency, at 0.92 gallons per 1,000 gross ton-miles.\nProductivity improved 6% for the quarter, when measured by million gross ton-miles per employee, with overall rail employee headcount down 6.2% compared to last year.\n“The progress we continue to make on productivity has helped drive substantial growth in profitability, earnings, and free cash flow. Our goal is to be the best performing railroad in North America, and the entire CSX team is responding to that challenge,” Chief Executive Steve Angel said on the railroad’s earnings call.\nThe results and outlook for the rest of the year prompted the railroad to raise its revenue growth forecast to high single-digits, up from prior guidance of mid-single-digit growth.\n“As we move into 2027, we will be focused on ensuring we have sufficient resources across the network to support a growing business, which will include adding to our train and engine employee pipeline,” Angel said. “We remain committed to sizing our workforce to the needs of a safe, reliable, and efficient railroad.”\nOverall freight volume increased 5% for the quarter. Intermodal was up 7%, merchandise was up 3%, and coal was up 2%.\nThe intermodal volume growth was due entirely to domestic shipments, as international traffic declined slightly.\n“Reduced trucking availability, high truck rates, and elevated diesel costs continue to benefit our intermodal segment,” said Maryclare Kenney, the railroad’s chief commercial officer.\nNew service offerings are attracting truckloads off the highway, she said, including interline service with BNSF through Birmingham, Ala., and with CPKC through Myrtlewood, Ala. Double-stack operations, made possible by completion of the Howard Street Tunnel clearance project in Baltimore in the spring, have helped with volume growth in the I-95 Corridor.\nCSX has experienced congestion at its Fairburn terminal outside Atlanta and is making investments in track, switching, and processing capacity to handle increased intermodal volume.\nExport coal tonnage increased 7% for the quarter, while domestic coal tonnage declined 3% as increased metallurgical coal volume was not enough to offset a downturn in utility coal traffic.\nKey operations metrics declined for the quarter amid higher volume. Average train velocity was off by 2%, while terminal dwell increased 12%, compared to a year ago.\nThe railroad’s service metrics declined compared to a year ago, with intermodal and carload trip-plan compliance falling 10 points each. Intermodal on-time performance was 83% for the quarter, while carload was 73%.\n“We are handling more volume safely and efficiently, but we know our network can perform better,” Angel said.\nAngel praised former Chief Operating Officer Mike Cory, who announced his retirement on Oct. 12. The railroad’s two operations senior vice presidents, Doug Ryhorchuk and Casey Albright, now report directly to Angel.\n“Most people think of me as an operating CEO, and that’s what I like to do,” said Angel, who joined CSX a year ago after leading industrial gas company Linde. “If you go back into my early days, I actually ran manufacturing operations for GE, and I installed one of the first lean production systems in the United States back in Nineties. So operations always has been in my blood.”\nSafety performance improved for the quarter, with the personal injury rate down 17% and the train accident rate down 5%. It was the seventh straight quarter of improvement in the injury rate, and the fifth consecutive quarter of improvement in the train accident rate.\nWhy it matters: CSX grew traffic and profits while spending less, but deteriorating service raised questions about whether its leaner operation could keep up.\nSubscribe to FreightWaves’ Rail e-newsletter and get the latest insights on rail freight right in your inbox.\nRead more:\nNew York short line wins $11.2 million federal rail grant\nAutonomous railcar technology earns $16M federal grant\nUnion Pacific CFO to retire\nCSX eliminates more than 100 management positions\nIntermodal rail better by 10% in latest count\nUpcoming FreightWaves Events\nCompliance\nBrokerage Compliance Symposium\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nAwards\nF3 Awards Dinner\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper of Choice winners revealed live. Cocktail reception into dinner and live music - 300 industry leaders in one purpose-built room.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nFreightTech\nF3: Future of Freight Festival\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nOctober 27, 2026 – October 28, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nCompliance Brokerage Compliance Symposium Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now\nAwards F3 Awards Dinner Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper of Choice winners revealed live. Cocktail reception into dinner and live music - 300 industry leaders in one purpose-built room.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now\nFreightTech F3: Future of Freight Festival Oct 27 – Oct 28 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now",
